{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>For</b> — the sense: 'that You have it to bestow good upon the wicked — You do not desire,' because he mentioned 'my King.'" "<b>Evil shall not sojourn with You</b> — יגורך [sojourn with You], like ישכבנה. Likewise גדלני [has made me great]: גדל עמי [has grown great with me]. And ורע הוא [and he is evil] is a phrase in which רע means 'a man' — it signifies nothing [i.e., is an idiom]. The witness: 'the boorish fools shall not stand' — from 'those who mock before Your eyes,' in the manner of men of the earth."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {1 "The phrase ורע הוא is hyper-literally rendered; Ibn Ezra appears to be addressing a phrase from rabbinic Hebrew (possibly from the Sages' language) where רע means 'a fellow/companion,' and his point about its idiomatic status is compressed and somewhat obscure."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:26:20.377866147Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}